.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 16, 2026
.Dt KISSBUFD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kissbufd
.Nd ACKMODE transmit buffer manager for KISS TNCs without flow control
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl DfnRSVhv
.Op Fl c Ar config
.Op Fl s Ar device
.Op Fl B Ar bytes
.Op Fl b Ar baud
.Op Fl m Ar mode
.Op Fl F Ar percent
.Op Fl p Ar link
.Op Fl t Ar seconds
.Op Fl r Ar seconds
.Op Fl Q Ar bytes
.Op Fl P Ar pidfile
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sits between a host KISS program such as
.Xr kissattach 8
and a TNC that supports the KISS ACKMODE extension (command 0x0C) but has
no hardware flow control, such as the NinoTNC.
It exposes a pseudo-terminal to the host and talks to the TNC over a
serial port, keeping the TNC transmit buffer as full as possible without
overflowing it.
.Pp
Every data frame is sent to the TNC as an ACKMODE frame carrying a unique
16-bit ID from an internal counter.
The TNC returns that ID when the frame has left over the air.
.Nm
keeps a running total of the raw KISS bytes (FEND delimiters, type byte,
ID, escapes and payload) of frames still on the TNC, and only releases the
next frame when it fits within the fill limit
.Pq Ar percent No of the buffer size .
Frames larger than the fill limit are dropped as giants.
.Pp
Plain KISS data frames from the host are tracked internally and their
ACKs are not relayed.
ACKMODE frames from the host keep their ID in a translation table; the
ACK is relayed to the host with the host's original ID.
Wire IDs never collide with a frame still in flight and never contain the
bytes 0xC0 or 0xDB.
IDs are handled in network byte order: the first byte on the wire is the
most significant.
.Pp
Other KISS commands (TXDELAY, persistence, SetHardware, ...) are passed
through unchanged, in order of arrival relative to data frames.
Frames received from the TNC are relayed unchanged; ACKs for unknown IDs
are discarded.
.Pp
If an ACK has not arrived within the ACK timeout, the frame is considered
lost and a minor alarm is logged; nothing is sent to the host.
If the serial link is lost (USB unplug, device error), all frames on the
TNC are considered lost, frames queued on the host side are held, and the
device is reopened periodically.
.Pp
Every setting can also be given in a configuration file, see
.Xr kissbufd.conf 5 .
Command line options override the file.
.Sh OPTIONS
The configuration file key of each option is given in brackets.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar config
Read settings from
.Ar config .
.It Fl n
Check the configuration, print a summary and exit.
.It Fl s Ar device
TNC serial device.
.Bq device
.It Fl B Ar bytes
TNC transmit buffer size, in raw KISS bytes, 64 to 1048576.
.Bq buffer_size
.It Fl b Ar baud
Serial speed; default 57600.
.Bq baud
.It Fl m Ar mode
Serial framing as data bits, parity and stop bits, e.g.
.Cm 8N1
(default),
.Cm 7E1
or
.Cm 8N2 .
.Bq serial_mode
.It Fl R
Drop DTR and RTS when opening the port; by default they are asserted.
.Bq dtr = no
.It Fl F Ar percent
Fill the TNC buffer up to this share of the buffer size; default 95.
.Bq fill_percent
.It Fl p Ar link
Create a symbolic link to the pty slave at
.Ar link ,
replacing an existing symlink and creating missing parent directories.
It is removed on exit.
.Bq pty_link
.It Fl t Ar seconds
ACK timeout; default 60.
.Bq ack_timeout
.It Fl r Ar seconds
Serial reopen interval after link loss; default 2.
.Bq reopen_interval
.It Fl Q Ar bytes
Limit of the host-side queue of frames waiting for room on the TNC.
Frames arriving while it is full are dropped; default 262144.
.Bq queue_limit
.It Fl D
Detach and run as a daemon; implies syslog logging.
.Bq daemon = yes
.It Fl f
Stay in the foreground, overriding
.Cm daemon
in the configuration file.
.Bq daemon = no
.It Fl S
Log to
.Xr syslog 3
(facility daemon) instead of standard error.
.Bq syslog = yes
.It Fl P Ar pidfile
Write the process ID to
.Ar pidfile ;
it is removed on exit.
.Bq pidfile
.It Fl v
Increase verbosity: once for informational messages, twice for per-frame
debug output.
.Bq verbose
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.El
.Pp
In the foreground, the pty slave name is printed on standard output at
startup.
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width SIGTERM
.It Dv SIGUSR1
Log statistics: frames, bytes, ACKs, timeouts, drops and peak buffer use.
.It Dv SIGTERM , Dv SIGINT
Exit cleanly.
.El
.Sh SERVICE MANAGEMENT
.Ss Linux (systemd)
A template unit runs one instance per configuration file
.Pa @SYSCONFDIR@/kissbufd/ Ns Ar instance Ns Pa .conf :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cp @DOCDIR@/kissbufd.conf.sample @SYSCONFDIR@/kissbufd/tnc0.conf
systemctl enable --now kissbufd@tnc0
.Ed
.Ss FreeBSD
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sysrc kissbufd_enable=YES
sysrc kissbufd_profiles="tnc0 tnc1"   # optional, one .conf each
service kissbufd start
.Ed
.Ss OpenBSD
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rcctl enable kissbufd
rcctl start kissbufd
.Ed
.Ss NetBSD
Set
.Va kissbufd=YES
in
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
.Ss macOS
.Bd -literal -offset indent
launchctl bootstrap system /Library/LaunchDaemons/kissbufd.plist
.Ed
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa @SYSCONFDIR@/kissbufd/*.conf
configuration files
.It Pa @DOCDIR@/kissbufd.conf.sample
annotated sample configuration
.It Pa @RUNDIR@/kissbufd/
pty symlinks and pid files used by the service files
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on normal exit, 1 on a runtime error, 2 on a usage or configuration
error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A TNC with a 32 kB buffer, attached as AX.25 port
.Dq radio :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kissbufd -s /dev/ttyACM0 -B 32768 -p @RUNDIR@/kissbufd/tnc0 &
kissattach @RUNDIR@/kissbufd/tnc0 radio
.Ed
.Pp
The same from a configuration file, with debug output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kissbufd -c @SYSCONFDIR@/kissbufd/tnc0.conf -vv
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kissbufd.conf 5 ,
.Xr kissattach 8
.Sh BUGS
A TNC reset that does not drop the serial link cannot be detected; frames
lost that way are reclaimed by the ACK timeout.
